---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About ShouldISpend"
  description="Learn why ShouldISpend exists, how its calculators work, and why the site gives straight, conservative verdicts on real spending decisions."
>
  <style>
    .about-page {
      max-width: 980px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 82px 24px 24px;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      color: var(--primary);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 1.8px;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    h1 {
      max-width: 850px;
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: clamp(2.8rem, 6vw, 5rem);
      line-height: 0.98;
      letter-spacing: -0.05em;
      margin: 0 0 24px;
    }

    h2 {
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.55rem);
      line-height: 1.08;
      letter-spacing: -0.035em;
      margin: 0 0 18px;
    }

    p,
    li {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 1.06rem;
      line-height: 1.85;
    }

    p {
      margin: 0 0 22px;
    }

    a {
      color: var(--primary);
      font-weight: 800;
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    a:hover {
      text-decoration: underline;
    }

    .intro {
      max-width: 820px;
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: 1.22rem;
      line-height: 1.75;
    }

    .panel {
      background: var(--card);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 24px;
      padding: 30px;
      margin-top: 34px;
      box-shadow: var(--shadow-soft);
    }

    .section {
      margin-top: 58px;
    }

    .grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
      gap: 22px;
      margin-top: 24px;
    }

    .card {
      background: var(--card);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 20px;
      padding: 24px;
    }

    .card h3 {
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: 1.18rem;
      line-height: 1.3;
      margin: 0 0 12px;
    }

    .card p {
      font-size: 0.98rem;
      line-height: 1.75;
      margin: 0;
    }

    .founder {
      background: var(--primary-dark);
      border-color: var(--primary-dark);
      color: #ffffff;
    }

    .founder h2 {
      color: #ffffff;
    }

    .founder p {
      color: #e7eadf;
    }

    .signature {
      color: #ffffff;
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-top: 6px;
    }

    .cta {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 12px;
      margin-top: 24px;
    }

    .button,
    .button-secondary {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      border-radius: 999px;
      padding: 12px 20px;
      font-weight: 800;
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    .button {
      background: var(--primary);
      color: #ffffff;
      border: 1px solid var(--primary);
    }

    .button-secondary {
      background: var(--card);
      color: var(--primary);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
    }

    .button:hover,
    .button-secondary:hover {
      text-decoration: none;
      transform: translateY(-2px);
    }

    @media (max-width: 700px) {
      .about-page {
        padding: 58px 20px 12px;
      }

      .panel,
      .card {
        padding: 22px;
      }

      .intro {
        font-size: 1.08rem;
      }

      .button,
      .button-secondary {
        width: 100%;
      }
    }
  </style>

  <div class="about-page">
    <section>
      <div class="eyebrow">About ShouldISpend</div>

      <h1>Money decisions need more than a rule of thumb.</h1>

      <p class="intro">
        ShouldISpend helps people pressure-test the spending decisions that look simple
        until real life gets involved: rent, groceries, weddings, medical bills, cars,
        college, baby costs, funerals, travel, debt, and emergency savings.
      </p>

      <div class="panel founder">
        <h2>Why I built ShouldISpend</h2>
        <p>
          I built ShouldISpend for the kind of money decisions that do not fit neatly
          inside a generic budgeting rule. A wedding, a car payment, a medical bill,
          a baby, a funeral - the question is not just whether you can pay for it today.
          It is whether it still feels manageable after the money is gone.
        </p>
        <p>
          I wanted a site that gives a straight verdict instead of a lecture. Not a perfect
          financial plan. Not a magic answer. Just a clearer way to see the tradeoff before
          you commit to something big.
        </p>
        <div class="signature">Dustin Baker, founder</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="eyebrow">How the site thinks</div>
      <h2>Price is only one part of the decision.</h2>

      <p>
        A $700 car payment can be fine for one household and a problem for another.
        A $30,000 wedding can be manageable in one situation and reckless in another.
        The number alone does not answer the question.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is why ShouldISpend calculators look at the surrounding context: income,
        savings, debt, monthly obligations, outside help, emergency cushion, payment plans,
        and what the decision leaves behind.
      </p>

      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Real-life inputs</h3>
          <p>
            The calculators are built around the details people actually juggle:
            income, savings, debt, family help, monthly bills, and the cost in front of them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Straight verdicts</h3>
          <p>
            Results are designed to be clear: go, wait, rethink it, or proceed carefully
            based on the pressure the decision creates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Conservative by design</h3>
          <p>
            If a decision leaves too little cushion, the site is supposed to say so.
            A cautious warning before you spend is better than regret after.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="eyebrow">What ShouldISpend is not</div>
      <h2>This is educational decision support, not financial advice.</h2>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend does not replace a financial advisor, tax professional, attorney,
        lender, doctor, insurance expert, or anyone who can evaluate your full personal
        situation. The calculators are educational tools built to help you think more
        clearly before making a spending decision.
      </p>

      <p>
        The goal is not to tell everyone to spend less. The goal is to show whether the
        decision fits your actual situation, what tradeoff it creates, and whether you
        still have enough room after the money leaves.
      </p>

      <div class="cta">
        <a class="button" href="/calculators/">Browse Calculators</a>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/methodology/">See the Methodology &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
